import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
import Note from '../components/Note.astro';
---

<Doc
  title="The three shapes"
  kicker="Chapter 02 — the taxonomy"
  description="Every repository is an atom, an assembly or an application. The taxonomy is enforced at the source, in every language ecosystem."
  path="/shapes"
>
  <p class="lede">
    Modularity is not reviewed after the fact — it is enforced by the shape of the
    repository. Every repository must fit exactly one of three archetypes. There is
    no fourth option and no hybrid.
  </p>

  <h2>1 — Atom</h2>

  <p>
    The smallest possible unit of granularity. If a component can be broken down
    further, it is not an atom.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Structure:</strong> flat. A single level of directory inside the source root.</li>
    <li><strong>Rule:</strong> no subdivisions. It fulfils one specific contract.</li>
    <li><strong>Test:</strong> can you describe it with one sentence that has no “and”?</li>
    <li><strong>Examples:</strong> a value object, a string utility, a single HTTP client, a contract-only package.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2 — Assembly</h2>

  <p>
    A connection point. Assemblies contain no logic; they orchestrate the union of
    atoms so that one dependency declaration installs ten packages.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Structure:</strong> no source code. Composition is declared in the package manifest.</li>
    <li><strong>Rule:</strong> if it has behaviour, it is not an assembly.</li>
    <li><strong>Test:</strong> can you delete its manifest and have nothing left but a README?</li>
    <li><strong>Examples:</strong> a meta-package that bundles the HTTP atoms, a “commons” bundle.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3 — Application</h2>

  <p>
    The living system. Not a library, but the implementation of the business heart,
    following Clean Architecture and DDD.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Structure:</strong> domain, application, infrastructure, interface — dependencies pointing inwards.</li>
    <li><strong>Rule:</strong> stay lean. Anything generic is pushed down into atoms.</li>
    <li><strong>Test:</strong> does it contain a use case that a business expert would recognize?</li>
    <li><strong>Examples:</strong> a payment gateway, an identity service, a kanban board.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The same idea in four ecosystems</h2>

  <p>
    The names of the artifacts change; the shapes do not.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Shape</th>
          <th>PHP / Composer</th>
          <th>C# / NuGet</th>
          <th>Java / Maven</th>
          <th>C++ / Conan</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Atom</strong></td>
          <td><code>type: library</code>, one namespace</td>
          <td>class library, one namespace</td>
          <td>jar, one package</td>
          <td>header + library, one namespace</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Assembly</strong></td>
          <td><code>type: metapackage</code>, only <code>require</code></td>
          <td>package with no compiled code, <code>PackageReference</code> only</td>
          <td>pom with <code>&lt;dependency&gt;</code> only, no sources</td>
          <td>conanfile that only <code>requires</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Application</strong></td>
          <td><code>type: project</code>, <code>src/Domain</code>, <code>src/Application</code>…</td>
          <td>executable / web project with layer folders</td>
          <td>bootable app with layer modules</td>
          <td>executable target with layer directories</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How to classify a repository</h2>

  <p>Ask the three questions in order and stop at the first “yes”.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Question</th><th>If yes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Does it contain use cases and business rules?</td><td>Application</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Does it contain only dependency declarations?</td><td>Assembly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Does it fit in one sentence with one responsibility?</td><td>Atom</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    If none of the answers is “yes”, the repository is not ready to exist. That is
    not a stylistic judgement; it is the signal that the boundary has not been found
    yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Why three, and not more</h2>

  <p>
    More shapes mean more places to hide. A fourth archetype — “shared utilities”,
    “common”, “core” — always becomes the place where unrelated things accumulate
    until nobody can delete anything.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three shapes also give a mechanical rule for leaks: code that belongs to an atom
    but lives in an application will eventually be copied into another application.
    The taxonomy makes that leak visible at review time, because the reviewer can
    ask a single question: <em>does this file belong to this shape?</em>
  </p>

  <Note label="Anti-pattern" variant="warn">
    <p>
      A repository called <code>utils</code>, <code>shared</code> or <code>core</code>
      with more than one responsibility. It is not an assembly (it has logic) and it
      is not an atom (it does several things). It is the shape that OTAKU removes.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <h2>Enforcement</h2>

  <p>
    A taxonomy that lives in a document is a suggestion. To enforce it, put the shape
    in machine-readable metadata and check it in CI:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>PHP:</strong> <code>composer.json</code> → <code>type</code> must be <code>library</code>, <code>metapackage</code> or <code>project</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>C#:</strong> an assembly must have no <code>.cs</code> files; the <code>.csproj</code> should be <code>Microsoft.NET.Sdk</code> with no compile items.</li>
    <li><strong>Java:</strong> an assembly module must have an empty <code>src/main/java</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>C++:</strong> an assembly is headers-only or absent; the <code>conanfile</code> must declare <code>requires</code> and no <code>sources()</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For the application shape, the check is structural: assert that the domain layer
    has no import of infrastructure, framework or database packages. Every ecosystem
    has a tool for that — <code>deptrac</code> (PHP), <code>NetArchTest</code> (C#),
    <code>ArchUnit</code> (Java), <code>include-what-you-use</code> plus build-graph
    rules (C++).
  </p>

